Here is the full **testsmells.org 19-smell academic catalog** audit of `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`, with integration-test calibration applied (the class is marked `[TestCategory("Integration")]`).

---

## Summary Dashboard

```
┌──────────┬─────────────┬────────────────┐
│ Severity │ Smell Count │ Affected Tests │
├──────────┼─────────────┼────────────────┤
│ High     │ 2           │ 2              │
│ Medium   │ 2           │ 2              │
│ Low      │ 0           │ 0              │
│ Total    │ 4           │ 4 / 8          │
└──────────┴─────────────┴────────────────┘
```

---

## Findings by Severity

### ❶ Conditional Test Logic — **High**

> *Academic name: Conditional Test Logic (testsmells.org)*

**Test:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 94)
**Why it's High even for integration tests:** Control-flow smells are never downgraded — the `if/else` means one branch is never exercised per run, hiding a testing gap regardless of test scope.

```csharp
// SMELL — one branch always wins; the other is dead coverage
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Fix — split into two explicit tests:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_StandardUser_ReturnsName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = (PremiumUser)_repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com")!;

    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Whichever branch does *not* execute is invisible — a regression in that path will never be caught.

---

### ❷ Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test) — **High**

> *Academic name: Unknown Test / Empty Test (testsmells.org)*

**Test:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113)

```csharp
// SMELL — 100 inserts run, but nothing is verified
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // No assertion
}
```

The name implies a "does not throw" intent, but MSTest will report this as passing even if every insert silently corrupts data.

**Fix — assert the observable outcome:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — the test passes even if inserts are silently dropped, truncated, or corrupted.

---

### ❸ Sleepy Test — **Medium** *(downgraded from High; integration calibration)*

> *Academic name: Sleepy Test (testsmells.org)*

**Test:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 82)

```csharp
// SMELL — hard-coded 3-second sleep
Thread.Sleep(3000);
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

Integration tests sometimes need to wait for asynchronous side-effects, which is why this is downgraded. But a fixed sleep is still non-deterministic (too short on slow CI, wastefully long on fast machines).

**Fix — poll with a timeout:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var notified = await PollUntilAsync(
        () => _repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        timeout: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5),
        interval: TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(100));

    Assert.IsTrue(notified, "Notification was not sent within the timeout.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky failures on slow CI runners; 3 seconds of wasted wall-clock time per run even when the event fires in 50 ms.

---

### ❹ Assertion Roulette — **Medium**

> *Academic name: Assertion Roulette (testsmells.org, extended catalog)*

**Test:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (line 28)

```csharp
// SMELL — 4 assertions, none with a diagnostic message
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

When one of these fails, the MSTest output says *"Assert.AreEqual failed. Expected: Alice. Actual: (null)."* — but with four assertions the developer must read the stack trace to find which line. At 4 assertions, this crosses the ≥ 3 threshold.

**Fix — add message parameters:**

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert.");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip.");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip.");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated Id should be positive.");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower failure diagnosis — especially in CI logs where you can't click through to the line.

---

## Smells Scanned — No Findings (15 / 19 clean)

| # | Smell | Verdict |
|---|-------|---------|
| 2 | **Mystery Guest** | ✅ Clean — uses in-memory SQLite (`:memory:`), a hermetic resource. No disk, network, or env-var coupling. |
| 5 | **Eager Test** | ✅ Clean — no test calls 4+ distinct production methods. Multi-method calls (Insert → GetByEmail) are standard arrange-act-assert for integration scope. |
| 6 | **Magic Number Test** | ✅ Clean — literals like `3` (line 76) and `10` (line 132) appear immediately after inserting that exact count. Contextually self-documenting. |
| 7 | **Sensitive Equality** | ✅ Clean — no `ToString()` / `string` conversion comparisons. |
| 8 | **Exception Handling** | ✅ Clean — no `try`/`catch` blocks. |
| 9 | **General Fixture** | ✅ Clean — both `_connection` and `_repository` are used by all 8 tests. |
| 10 | **Ignored / Disabled Test** | ✅ Clean — no `[Ignore]` or skip markers. |
| 11 | **Duplicate Assert** | ✅ Clean — no repeated identical assertions. |
| 12 | **Lazy Test** | ✅ Clean — shared use of `Insert` is arrange-step setup, not redundant testing of the same behavior. |
| 13 | **Constructor Initialization** | ✅ Clean — correctly uses `[TestInitialize]`. |
| 14 | **Default Test** | ✅ Clean — class is meaningfully named. |
| 15 | **Redundant Print** | ✅ Clean — no `Console.WriteLine` or logging noise. |
| 16 | **Redundant Assertion** | ✅ Clean — no tautological assertions. |
| 17 | **Resource Optimism** | ✅ Clean — in-memory DB is always available. |
| 18 | **Empty Test** | ✅ Clean — no entirely empty bodies. |

---

## Smell-Free Patterns Worth Highlighting

- **`InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity`** — tests a genuine integration concern (thread safety) with a clear assertion on observable state. Well-designed.
- **`DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase`** — textbook arrange-act-assert with a single focused assertion.
- **`ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted`** — good use of `CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreNotNull` to go beyond count-checking.
- **Test fixture** — in-memory SQLite with per-test `[TestInitialize]`/`[TestCleanup]` is a best-practice pattern that avoids Mystery Guest entirely.

---

## Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Impact | Effort |
